BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2373@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T102220Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T100000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T104500
SUMMARY:Kernel CVEs at AWS Scale: Two Years of Empirical Findings
LOCATION:South Hall 1 B\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Kernel Summit Track · Dylan Johnson\, Justinien Bouron · http
 s://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2373/\n\nSince the Linux kernel proj
 ect became a CVE Numbering Authority (CNA) in February 2024\, organization
 s maintaining custom kernels have faced a flood of CVE disclosures.\n\nWe 
 present empirical findings\, lessons learned\, and the key challenges that
  the kernel team responsible for Amazon Web Services fleet infrastructure 
 encountered over the following two years while handling the steady flow of
  kernel CVEs.\n\nWe summarize our approach to assessing kernel CVEs in a w
 arehouse-scale environment\, pruning our code base to reduce the attack su
 rface\, along with quantitative results from field data. Specifically\, we
  examine\n\n - how NVD/CVSS scores and third-party assessments correlate w
 ith our final in-house ratings\;\n - how community guidance\, including st
 able tree tracking and domain-specific risk assessment\, measurably improv
 ed our development velocity\, kernel maintenance\, and kernel adoption str
 ategy\; and\n - how backporting practices introduce regressions and compou
 nd risk.\n\nWe then present data on patching velocity and CVE-related tren
 ds across LTS versions. Although our data comes from a single environment\
 , we believe these findings generalize to any organization that maintains 
 in-house or custom kernels.\n\nWe will close with an open discussion on em
 erging developments (including where AI-assisted tooling fits into the CVE
  triage and patching pipeline) and invite attendees to challenge\, extend\
 , or contradict our findings with their own data.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2373/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2372@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T102220Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T104500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T113000
SUMMARY:Regressions & tracking them: current state\, plans\, and what do yo
 u want?
LOCATION:South Hall 1 B\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Kernel Summit Track · Thorsten Leemhuis · https://lpc.events/
 event/20/contributions/2372/\n\nProvide a quick "state of the union" about
  the Linux kernel regression ecosystem in the first part of the session be
 fore spending the second discussing what improvement the members of the au
 dience wish for in this area.\n\nThe first part is meant to take less then
  half of the allotted time and will cover things like:\n\n* KernelCI and r
 egzbot joined forces -- what this means for the future.\n* Tracking regres
 sions with regzbot\, the regression tracking bot: status and future plans.
 \n* Linux development workflow patters that lead to regressions or delay r
 esolving them.\n\nThe second half will be audience driven and might discus
 s details in the areas raised earlier\, things like the following\, or wha
 tever the audience is interested it:\n\n* Regression tracking with regzbot
  still useful in the age of AI?\n* How to improve interaction with parties
  interested in the space of CI\, regressions\, and tracking them – like 
 kernel maintainers of distros that regularly update their kernels to lates
 t stable or longterm series.\n* Is there interest in trees with "pending" 
 and "wip" regression fixes?\n\nIn case I'm invited to the kernel maintaine
 rs summit I'll also collect topics with regards to regressions the audienc
 e wants me to bring up at kernel maintainer summit a few days later.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2372/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2375@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T102220Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T120000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T124500
SUMMARY:Challenges in multi-tenant GPU sharing
LOCATION:South Hall 1 B\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Kernel Summit Track · Boqun Feng\, Gregoire Pean\, Jatin Katar
 ia · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2375/\n\nWe would like to 
 share some of the challenges we have encountered in a multi-tenant GPU env
 ironment\, discuss the solutions we have explored\, and gather feedback fr
 om the community.\n\nTo provide context for the audience\, we will start b
 y giving an overview of our multi-tenant architecture. We then plan to dis
 cuss several areas where we have encountered challenges\, including:\n\n  
 - GPU reset and debugging\n  - Identifying the problematic tenant session 
 among many concurrent sessions\n  - Missing resource management capabiliti
 es\, especially around memory cgroups\n  - Lock contention in drivers\n  -
  Power distribution across different system components\n\nWe would like to
  close the session with an open discussion on the issues and solutions pre
 sented. If the right audience is present\, we would also like to discuss w
 hat a reasonable roadmap could look like for improving Linux support for m
 ulti-tenant GPU platforms.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2375/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2370@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T102220Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T124500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T133000
SUMMARY:Maintainer-oriented features of b4
LOCATION:South Hall 1 B\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Kernel Summit Track · Konstantin Ryabitsev · https://lpc.even
 ts/event/20/contributions/2370/\n\nB4 is already a well-known tool for ret
 rieving and applying series from lore.kernel.org. Recently\, it gained sev
 eral new features that can make the life of a maintainer a bit easier:\n\n
 - b4 review - helps with code review and full series lifecycle management\
 n- b4 bugs - integrates distributed bug tracking into your workflow\n\nThi
 s session will go over the new features and how they can assist overloaded
  maintainers in keeping a handle on the stream of incoming changes.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2370/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2371@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T102220Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T150000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T154500
SUMMARY:Rust for Linux
LOCATION:South Hall 1 B\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Kernel Summit Track · Miguel Ojeda · https://lpc.events/event
 /20/contributions/2371/\n\n[Rust for Linux](https://rust-for-linux.com) is
  the project adding support for the Rust language to the Linux kernel. Thi
 s talk will give a high-level overview of the status and the latest news a
 round Rust in the kernel since LPC 2025.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2371/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2376@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T102220Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T154500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T163000
SUMMARY:Leveraging Rust's Field Projections in the Kernel and Beyond
LOCATION:South Hall 1 B\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Kernel Summit Track · Benno Lossin · https://lpc.events/event
 /20/contributions/2376/\n\nSeveral Rust contributors and I have been colla
 borating on a novel language feature called "Field Projections". The featu
 re is still being designed and implemented\, so now is a good opportunity 
 to experiment with it to see what new APIs it unlocks for the Kernel and o
 ther projects. We also want to investigate any gaps in our current design 
 that prevent important use-cases from being supported.\n\nRust makes heavy
  use of custom pointers filling the gap (& going beyond) between reference
 s (`&T` and `&mut T`) and raw pointers (`*const T` and `*mut T`). Currentl
 y\, they have two major issues: ergonomics and feature parity with builtin
  references and `Box<T>`. Raw pointers and their extensions (e.g. `NonNull
 <T>`) have especially bad ergonomics. Our Field Projection language featur
 e aims to remedy these shortcomings of custom (dumb & smart) pointers\; ou
 r current approach is an ambitious generalization of `Deref` that supports
  a plethora of custom pointers from `MyBox<T>` (that has all the propertie
 s of `Box<T>`) to `VolatilePtr<T>` (which is only using `{read\,write}_vol
 atile` to access the pointee). Our proposal integrates tightly with existi
 ng features such as place expressions\, operations on places\, the borrow-
 checker\, and autoref.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2376/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2374@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T102220Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T174500
SUMMARY:DRM: handling runtime requirements for device components.
LOCATION:South Hall 1 B\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Kernel Summit Track · Dmitry Baryshkov · https://lpc.events/e
 vent/20/contributions/2374/\n\nThe DRM susbsytem grew up from the desktop 
 GPUs\, where the device is a single unit\, powered on and off only at the 
 important runtime points. For the embedded display controllers it's no lon
 ger true. The display pipeline can consist of several different devices\, 
 each having its own runtime power up and down code points. Handling device
  power on and off in the existing atomic callbacks makes the code fragile:
  it's too easy to create a disbalance of calls or to miss a register acces
 s from one of the points.\n\nIn this talk I would like to point out these 
 issues and trigger a discussion about possible ways to solve the issue.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2374/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2377@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T102220Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T174500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T183000
SUMMARY:Bringing Linux DRM Display Panel support in the modern age
LOCATION:South Hall 1 B\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Kernel Summit Track · Neil Armstrong · https://lpc.events/eve
 nt/20/contributions/2377/\n\nSince the introduction of the first Samsung D
 SI panel\, the Linux DRM panel API has been a crucial piece of software fo
 r enabling displays across diverse architectures\, but it has not evolved 
 alongside modern graphics stacks. Currently\, the API lacks atomic DRM API
  support and the ability to adapt power setups during mode changes. Furthe
 rmore\, it fails to support advanced Display Driver IC (DDIC) features tha
 t modern hardware heavily relies on\, including:\n- Standby and advanced p
 ower states\n- Advanced color management\n- Dynamic rate switching\n- Comm
 and mode self-refresh\n\nThis lack of evolution has led to severe fragment
 ation between upstream and vendor downstream trees for advanced devices su
 pport\, creating a heavy maintenance burden and making native hardware sup
 port incredibly difficult.\n\nThe goal would be to outline these architect
 ural limitations and trigger a discussion on how to collaboratively modern
 ize the panel API. By standardizing advanced DDIC capabilities and fully e
 mbracing the atomic DRM API\, we hope to establish a unified path forward 
 for the entire Linux community.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2377/
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
